<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Fiennes, 1st Viscount Saye and Sele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1582–1662</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Lord Saye and Sele</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Fiennes, 1st Viscount Saye and Sele, was an English nobleman and Puritan leader who opposed Charles I's prerogative taxation. He invested in colonial ventures including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-island-company">Providence Island Company</a> and the Saybrook settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, which was partly named after him. During the Civil War he sided with Parliament, though he made his peace with Charles II at the Restoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-fiennes-1st-viscount-saye-and-sele.jpg" alt="William Fiennes, 1st Viscount Saye and Sele" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wenceslaus Hollar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LordSayeAndSele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
